use web
use json
use core
use md
into chat.he
lang he

The module is namespace `chat`. Text parameters are read-only byte views. A function that produces HTML or JSON text appends to a growable byte buffer `out` that is its first parameter. An element is an i32 handle from the web module; `web::el` gives the same handle for the same element and 0 when there is no such element. A handle the module created or queried for itself and no longer needs is passed to `web::release`; handles of fixed page elements are never released.

The tags are i32 constants: `T_INPUT` is 100, `T_KEYDOWN` 101, `T_SEND` 102, `T_INTERRUPT` 103, `T_STEER` 104, `T_NEW` 105, `T_HISTORY` 106, `T_DIFF` 107 and `T_MSGS` 108 (listeners on fixed page elements), `T_STREAM` 110 (the chat stream), `T_UNDO` 111, `T_ACCEPT` 112, `T_IGNORE` 113 and `T_STEERED` 114 (results of fetches), `T_CONFIRM_UNDO` 115 and `T_CONFIRM_CLEAR` 116 (results of the browser's confirm call), `T_COPY_RESET` 117 (a timer), `T_FMT` 118 and `T_TIME` 119 (results of js calls).

The module variables, all empty, zero or false at the start unless a value is given: `session` is a growable byte buffer with the id of the chat session, empty when there is none. `current_id` is an int, the id of the current chat, 0 when there is none. `new_title` is a growable byte buffer. `stream_id` is an i32, the request id of the running chat stream, 0 when none. `sbuf` is a growable byte buffer of received stream bytes not yet parsed. `final_msg` is a growable byte buffer and `got_message` a bool. `tools_json` and `widgets_json` are growable byte buffers holding the JSON texts of the objects collected during one turn, separated by commas and without brackets; `n_tools` and `n_widgets` are ints counting them. `diff_text` is a growable byte buffer with the diff entries as JSON array text, `dd` is a json::Doc parsed from it and `droot` is an int with its root node, -1 when there is none. `hd`, `ed`, `td`, `wd` and `pd` are json::Doc values. `copy_q` is a growable list of i32 handles with `copy_head` an int, the index of its oldest unused entry. `date_fmt` is an i32 handle, 0 until it is known. `time_ids` and `time_els` are growable lists of i32. `steer_ids` is a growable list of i32, `steer_ends` a growable list of int and `steer_texts` a growable byte buffer.

`str_of(d, n, key)` is the string value of the member `key` of node `n` of the json::Doc `d`, or an empty view when `n` is negative or the member is missing or is not a string.

`utf16_len(s)` is the number of UTF-16 code units JavaScript counts in the UTF-8 text `s`: every character counts 1, except a character of four bytes, which counts 2. `utf16_cut(s, n)` is the longest prefix of `s`, as a view and never ending inside a character, that holds at most `n` UTF-16 code units.

`line_count(s)` is the number of pieces JavaScript's split on a newline gives for `s`: one more than the number of newline bytes, so an empty text gives 1. `line_at(s, k)` is piece number `k`, counting from 0, of that split, as a view of `s`, or an empty view when `k` is past the end.

`scroll_down()` sets the number property "scrollTop" of the element "messages" to its number property "scrollHeight" (`web::get_num` and `web::prop_num`).

`set_generating(on)` sets the boolean property "disabled" with `web::prop_bool` to `on` on the element "send" and to the opposite on the elements "interrupt" and "steer".

`focus_input()` calls the method "focus" of the element "input" with `web::call`.

`auto_resize()` sets the style "height" of the element "input" to "auto", then to the smaller of its number property "scrollHeight" and 150, as a whole number followed by "px".

`toggle_diff()` toggles the class "open" of the element "diff-col" and the class "active" of the element "diff-toggle", each with `web::cls` using the opposite of what `web::has_cls` says.

`chat_entry(out, title)` appends the JSON object of the current chat with the members "id" (`current_id`), "title" (`title`), "date" (the current time in milliseconds from `web::epoch_ms`, as a whole number), "html" (the property "innerHTML" of the element "messages", read with `web::get_prop`), "diff" (`diff_text`, or the text [] when it is empty) and "sessionId" (`session` as a JSON string, or null when it is empty).

`hist_load()` reads the local storage item "amniHistory" with `web::store_get` and parses it into `hd` with json::parse. It returns the root node, or -1 when the item is missing, empty, fails to parse or is not an array.

`save_chat()` does nothing when `current_id` is 0. It takes the root from `hist_load()` and looks in the array for the element whose "id" whole number equals `current_id`; that is `found`, and its "title" string is the old title. The title is the old title when found and not empty, else `new_title` when it is not empty, else "New Chat". It builds the new array text: "[", then, when not found, the `chat_entry` followed by a comma when the array has any element, then every element of the array in order separated by commas, each written with json::format_node except the found one, which is written with `chat_entry`, then "]". It stores the text with `web::store_set` under "amniHistory" and clears `new_title`.

`diff_count()` is the number of elements of `dd` from its root `droot`, 0 when `droot` is negative. `diff_clear()` clears `diff_text` and sets `droot` to -1. `diff_set(text)` copies `text` into a temporary buffer first, because it may be a view of `diff_text`, then replaces `diff_text` with it and parses it into `dd` with json::parse, storing the root in `droot`; when parsing fails or the root is not an array it calls `diff_clear`.

`diff_push(file, kind, removed, added)` appends one entry to the diff list. The entry is the JSON object with the members "file", "type" (`kind`), "removed" and "added", written with json::format_key and json::format_string. The new array text is built in a temporary buffer: when `diff_text` is empty it is "[", the entry and "]"; otherwise it is `diff_text` without its last byte, a comma, the entry and "]". It is stored with `diff_set`.

`diff_from_tool(d, n)` takes a json::Doc `d` and the node `n` of a tool result object, which has the string member "tool" and the object member "input" with the strings "path", "old_string", "new_string" and "content". The file is the input's "path", or "unknown" when that is empty. When the tool is "edit_file", the removed text is the input's "old_string" and the added text its "new_string"; when it is "write_file", the removed text is empty and the added text is the input's "content". It calls `diff_push` with the file, the tool, the removed and the added text, then `render_diff()`, then `save_chat()` when `current_id` is not 0. Use `str_of` for the strings.

`diff_icon(out, file)` appends an emoji chosen by the extension of `file`, which is the text after its last dot, or the whole `file` when it has no dot, lowercased as ASCII: "js" and "ts" give 📜, "py" gives 🐍, "rs" gives 🦀, "html" gives 🌐, "css" gives 🎨, "json" gives 📋, anything else gives 📄.

`diff_row(out, kind, num, sign, text)` appends the text `<div class="diff-row ` then `kind`, then `"><span class="diff-gutter">`, then the whole number `num`, then `</span><span class="diff-sign">`, then `sign`, then `</span><span class="diff-code">`, then `text` through `md::diff_line`, then `</span></div>`.

`diff_fold(out, count)` appends the text `<div class="diff-row ctx-fold"><span class="diff-gutter">` then three middle dots (the character U+00B7 three times), then `</span><span class="diff-sign"> </span><span class="diff-code" style="color:var(--dim);font-style:italic">`, then the whole number `count`, then ` unchanged lines</span></div>`.

`diff_section(out, file, kind, removed, added)` appends one section: `<div class="diff-section"><div class="diff-file-header">`, then `diff_icon` of `file`, a space, `<span class="diff-filename">`, `file` through `md::esc_html`, `</span>`, a space, the badge `<span class="diff-badge write">CREATE</span>` when `kind` is "write_file" and otherwise `<span class="diff-badge edit">EDIT</span>`, then `</div><div class="diff-lines">`, then the rows, then `</div></div>`. When `kind` is "write_file", the rows are, for every piece k of `added` taken with `line_count` and `line_at`, `diff_row(out, "add", k plus 1, "+", piece)`. For every other kind the rows are made like this. `old_n` is the number of pieces of `removed`, 0 when `removed` is empty, and `new_n` is the same for `added`. Let `i` be the count of leading positions where both pieces exist and are equal. Let `j1` be `old_n` minus 1 and `j2` be `new_n` minus 1, as signed ints that may be negative. While `j1` and `j2` are both at least `i` and old piece `j1` equals new piece `j2`, subtract 1 from both. Let `pre` be the larger of 0 and `i` minus 2. Then, in this order: when `pre` is above 0, `diff_fold(out, pre)`; for every k from `pre` up to but not including `i`, a context row, which is `diff_row(out, "ctx", k plus 1, " ", old piece k)`; for every k from `i` up to and including `j1`, `diff_row(out, "rm", k plus 1, "-", old piece k)`; for every k from `i` up to and including `j2`, `diff_row(out, "add", k plus 1, "+", new piece k)`; let `post` be the smaller of `old_n` minus 1 and `j1` plus 2; for every k from `j1` plus 1 up to and including `post`, a context row with old piece k; finally, when `j1` is less than `old_n` minus 3, `diff_fold(out, old_n minus j1 minus 3)`.

`render_diff()` fills the element "diff-body". When `diff_count()` is 0 it sets the html of "diff-body" to `<p style="color:var(--dim);padding:20px;text-align:center">No changes yet</p>` and stops. Otherwise it builds the html by calling `diff_section` for every element of `dd` in order, with that element's strings "file", "type", "removed" and "added" taken with `str_of`, sets the html of "diff-body" to it and then, when the element "diff-col" does not have the class "open", calls `toggle_diff()`.

`add_lines(out, text, sign, color, limit)` appends one div for each of the first `limit` pieces of `text` split on newlines (all pieces when `limit` is negative): `<div style="color:` then `color`, then `">`, then `sign`, then a space, then the piece through `md::esc_html`, then `</div>`. It returns the number of pieces of `text`, taken with `line_count` and `line_at`.

`json_pretty(out, d, n, depth)` appends node `n` of the json::Doc `d` the way JavaScript's JSON.stringify with an indent of 2 writes it. An object is "{", a newline, each member on its own line indented by 2 spaces per level as the key string, a colon, a space and the value, with a comma after every member except the last, then a newline, the indent of the outer level and "}". An array is the same with only the values and "[" and "]". An empty object is "{}" and an empty array "[]". `depth` is the current level, 0 at the top. Strings, numbers, booleans and null are written with json::format_node. A negative `n` is written as null.

`tool_detail(out, d, n)` appends the details of one tool result, node `n` of the json::Doc `d`, which has the string members "tool", "output" and "status" and the object member "input". Use `str_of`. When the tool is "edit_file" or "write_file", it first appends `<div style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text2)">&#9472;&#9472; `, the tool through `md::esc_html`, ` &#8250; <strong>`, the input's "path" through `md::esc_html`, and `</strong></div>`. For "edit_file" it then calls `add_lines` with the input's "old_string", sign "-", color "#ff5c5c" and no limit, then `add_lines` with the input's "new_string", sign "+", color "#3ddc84" and no limit. For "write_file" it then calls `add_lines` with the input's "content", sign "+", color "#3ddc84" and limit 30, and when the number it returns is above 30, appends `<div style="color:var(--dim)">... `, that number minus 30, and ` more lines</div>`. For any other tool it appends `<div style="margin:4px 0;color:var(--text2)">&#9472;&#9472; `, the tool through `md::esc_html`, ` &#9472;&#9472;</div>`, then `<div>`, the `json_pretty` of the input (starting at depth 0) cut with `utf16_cut` to 300 units and passed through `md::esc_html`, and `</div>`; and when the "output" string is not empty, `<div style="color:var(--dim)">`, the output cut to 300 units through `md::esc_html`, the text "..." when `utf16_len` of the output is above 300, and `</div>`.

`tool_group(out, d, arr)` appends the tool group for the array node `arr` of the json::Doc `d`: `<div class="tool-group">`, then for every tool the text `<span class="tool-badge ` followed by "success" when its "status" string is "success" and otherwise "error", then `">&#9889; `, the "tool" string through `md::esc_html`, and `</span>`; then `<button class="tool-toggle">&#9656; details</button><div class="tool-detail" style="display:none">`, then `tool_detail` for every tool, then `</div></div>`.

`add_msg(role, content, tools, widgets)` appends a message to the element "messages". `tools` and `widgets` are JSON array texts, empty when there are none. It first removes the element "welcome" when there is one, and releases its handle. It builds the html: when `role` is "user", it is `content` through `md::esc_html`. Otherwise, when `role` is "assistant" and `tools` is not empty, `tools` is parsed into `td` with json::parse, and when that works the html is the `tool_group` of `td` and its root followed by `md::render` of `content`, and then, for every tool of `td` whose "tool" string is "write_file" or "edit_file", `diff_from_tool` is called with `td` and that tool node; otherwise the html is `md::render` of `content`. When `role` is "assistant" and `widgets` is not empty, `widgets` is parsed into `wd` and for every element of `wd` the html of `md::widget` called with `out`, `wd` and that element's node is appended to the html. It creates a div with `web::create`, sets its attribute "class" to "msg " followed by `role`, sets its html, appends it to the element "messages", releases its handle, calls `scroll_down()`, and finally, when `current_id` is not 0, calls `save_chat()`.

`show_thinking()` creates a div with the attribute "class" "msg assistant" and the attribute "id" "thinking" whose html is `<div class="thinking"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>`, appends it to the element "messages", releases its handle and calls `scroll_down()`.

`update_thinking(text)` does nothing when there is no element "thinking". Otherwise it sets the html of "thinking" to `<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px"><div class="thinking"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><span style="font-size:12px;color:var(--dim)">`, then `text` through `md::esc_html`, then `</span></div>`.

`remove_thinking()` removes the element "thinking" from the page when there is one, and releases the handle of its ".live-steps" descendant when it has one, and the handle of "thinking" itself.

`live_icon(tool)` is a view of the icon text for the tool name: read_file gives ▤, write_file ✎, edit_file ✎, multi_edit ✎, run_command ›, list_directory ☰, search_files ⌕, web_fetch ⇣, web_search ⌕, run_tests ✓, run_lint ≋, run_format ¶, git_status ⎇, git_diff ±, git_add +, git_commit ●, git_log ≡, find_definition ⌖, and any other tool ⚙.

`live_arg(d, n)` is the first non-empty string among the members "path", "command", "query", "url", "name" and "message" of the object member "input" of node `n` of the json::Doc `d`, or an empty view. Use `str_of`.

`live_steps()` is the handle of the descendant with class "live-steps" of the element "thinking", or 0 when there is no element "thinking". When "thinking" exists but has no such descendant, it first sets the html of "thinking" to `<div class="live-head"><div class="thinking"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><span class="live-title">Working…</span></div><ol class="live-steps"></ol>` and queries again with `web::query`.

`live_title(text)` sets the text of the descendant with class "live-title" of the element "thinking" to `text` with `web::text`, when both exist.

`live_start(d, n)` takes the node `n` of a tool object in the json::Doc `d`, with the string member "tool", and returns the handle of the new step, or 0 when `live_steps()` is 0. The step is a "li" element created with `web::create`, with the attribute "class" "step run", the attribute "data-tool" set to the tool, and the html `<span class="st-ico">`, `live_icon` of the tool, `</span><span class="st-name">`, the tool through `md::esc`, `</span><span class="st-arg">`, `live_arg` through `md::esc`, and `</span><span class="st-state">running</span>`. It appends the step to the list, calls `live_title` with "Running " followed by the tool and the character … (U+2026), and calls `scroll_down()`.

`live_end(d, n)` takes the node `n` of a tool result in the json::Doc `d`, with the strings "tool", "status" and "output". It does nothing when `live_steps()` is 0. It finds, among the descendants of the list matching "li.step.run" (`web::query_all` and `web::list_at`), the first whose attribute "data-tool" equals the tool, releasing the handles of the others; when there is none it uses `live_start`. It removes the class "run" from it and adds the class "ok" when the status is "success", "plan" when it is "planned", otherwise "err". It sets the text of its ".st-state" descendant to "done" when the status is "success" and otherwise to the status. When the output is not empty, it creates a div with the attribute "class" "st-out" whose text is the output cut to 240 UTF-16 units with `utf16_cut`, followed by the character … (U+2026) when `utf16_len` of the output is above 240, and appends it to the step, releasing the div's handle. It calls `live_title` with "Thinking…" and releases the handle of the step.

`new_chat()` clears `session`, calls `diff_clear()`, sets `current_id` to 0, clears `new_title`, sets the html of the element "messages" to an empty text, and then calls `web::js_call` on the handle `web::js_global("location")` with the method "reload", the arguments text "[]" and the tag `T_IGNORE`.

`show_history()` calls `render_history()` and adds the class "open" to the elements "history-panel" and "settings-overlay".

`render_history()` reads `hist_load()`. When it is -1 or the array is empty, it sets the html of the element "history-list" to `<p style="color:var(--dim);font-size:12px;text-align:center">No recent chats</p>` and stops. Otherwise it sets that html to the following for every element in order, with `id` and `date` its whole numbers "id" and "date" (0 when missing) and the title its "title" string: `<div class="hist-item" data-act="chat.load" data-arg="`, `id`, `"><button class="hist-del" data-act="chat.delete" data-arg="`, `id`, `">&#10005;</button><div class="hist-title">`, the title through `md::esc_html`, `</div><div class="hist-time" data-ts="`, `date`, `"></div></div>`. Then it calls `fill_times()`.

`fill_times()` does nothing when `date_fmt` is 0. Otherwise, for every element matching ".hist-time" inside the element "history-list" (`web::query_all` and `web::list_at`) that has a non-empty attribute "data-ts", it calls `web::js_call` on `date_fmt` with the method "format", the arguments text made of "[", the attribute text and "]", and the tag `T_TIME`, and pushes the returned call id on `time_ids` and the element handle on `time_els`.

`on_time()` handles a `T_TIME` result: it looks up `web::ev_id()` in `time_ids`; when it is not there it stops. When `web::js_result` of that id gives a JSON string that json::parse reads into `ed`, it sets the text of the element handle at the same position of `time_els` to that string. It releases that handle and sets the id at that position to 0; when every id is 0 it clears both lists.

`load_chat(id)` reads `hist_load()` and looks for the array element whose "id" whole number equals `id`, and stops when there is none. It sets `current_id` to `id`, copies the element's "sessionId" string into `session` (empty when it is missing), sets the diff to the element's "diff" member written as text with json::format_node (`diff_clear()` when it is missing) using `diff_set`, and sets the html of the element "messages" to the element's "html" string. Then it deletes the attribute "onclick" from every element matching "[onclick]" inside "messages" (`web::query_all`, `web::list_at` and `web::del_attr`), calls `scroll_down()` and `render_diff()`, and removes the class "open" from the elements "history-panel" and "settings-overlay".

`delete_chat(id)` reads `hist_load()` and stores under "amniHistory" the array text of every element in order except the one whose "id" whole number equals `id`, written with json::format_node and separated by commas; then, when `id` equals `current_id` it calls `new_chat()`, and otherwise `render_history()`.

`clear_history()` calls `web::js_call` on `web::WINDOW` with the method "confirm", the arguments text made of "[" and the JSON string "Clear all chat history?" and "]", and the tag `T_CONFIRM_CLEAR`.

`confirmed()` is true when `web::js_result` of `web::ev_id()` gives the text "true".

`clear_history_go()` stores the text "[]" in local storage under "amniHistory", calls `new_chat()` and then `render_history()`.

`post_json(url, tag, body)` starts a `web::request` to `url` with the method "POST", the headers text `{"Content-Type":"application/json"}`, the `body`, the tag `tag` and a timeout of 0, and returns the request id.

`session_body(out)` appends the JSON object with the single member "session_id", the `session` text as a JSON string.

`handle_sse(event, data)` handles one stream event and returns false when the turn is over and the rest of the stream must be ignored, otherwise true. It parses `data` into `ed` with json::parse, and when that fails it calls `web::warn` with "SSE parse error:" and returns true. Let `root` be the parsed root. For the event "session": `session` becomes the "session_id" string of the root (empty when it is not a string). For "thinking": when the "text" string is not empty, `update_thinking` of it. For "tool_start": `live_start` with `ed` and the root, and release the returned handle when it is not 0. For "tool_result": append `data` to `tools_json`, after a comma when `n_tools` is above 0, add 1 to `n_tools`, call `live_end` with `ed` and the root, and when the "tool" string is "write_file" or "edit_file" call `diff_from_tool` with `ed` and the root. For "message": `final_msg` becomes the "message" string, and `got_message` becomes true. For "widget": append `data` to `widgets_json` the same way with `n_widgets`. For "error": call `remove_thinking()`, then `add_msg` with the role "system error", the "error" string, or "Unknown error" when it is empty, with a leading "Error: " removed, and empty tools and widgets, then `end_turn()`, then `stop_stream()`, and return false. For "interrupted": call `remove_thinking()`, then `add_msg` with the role "system", the content "Generation interrupted." and empty tools and widgets, then `end_turn()`, then `stop_stream()`, and return false. Any other event does nothing.

`end_turn()` calls `set_generating(false)` and `focus_input()`.

`stop_stream()` calls `web::fetch_abort` with `stream_id` and sets `stream_id` to 0.

`send()` takes the text of the property "value" of the element "input", trimmed, and does nothing when it is empty or when `stream_id` is not 0. When `current_id` is 0, it becomes the current time in milliseconds from `web::epoch_ms` as a whole number and `new_title` becomes the first 40 UTF-16 units of the text, taken with `utf16_cut`, followed by "...". Then it sets the property "value" of "input" to an empty text, calls `auto_resize()`, `set_generating(true)`, `add_msg` with the role "user", the text and empty tools and widgets, and `show_thinking()`. It clears `sbuf`, `final_msg`, `tools_json` and `widgets_json`, sets `got_message` to false and `n_tools` and `n_widgets` to 0. It builds a JSON object with the members "message" (the text), "session_id" (the `session` text as a JSON string, or null when `session` is empty) and "working_dir" (the property "value" of the element "cfg-dir", empty when there is no such element), and calls `web::stream` with the url "/api/chat", the method "POST", the headers text `{"Content-Type":"application/json"}`, that body and the tag `T_STREAM`; the returned id goes to `stream_id`.

`on_chunk()` handles one chunk of the chat stream. It takes the bytes of the chunk with `web::fetch_take` on `web::ev_id()` into a cleared buffer, then calls `web::fetch_free` on that id. When `web::ev_status()` equals `stream_id`, it appends the bytes to `sbuf` and then, while `core::sse_take` of `sbuf` returns true with an event name and data text, calls `handle_sse` for every event whose data is not empty, and stops the loop when `handle_sse` returns false.

`on_stream_end()` handles the end of the chat stream. It calls `web::fetch_free` on `web::ev_id()`. It does nothing more unless `web::ev_id()` equals `stream_id`; then it sets `stream_id` to 0, calls `remove_thinking()`, and when `web::ev_status()` is 0 it calls `add_msg` with the role "system", the content "Connection error &mdash; is the server running? Check Settings for provider config." and empty tools and widgets. Otherwise, when `got_message` is true or `n_tools` or `n_widgets` is above 0, it calls `add_msg` with the role "assistant", `final_msg`, the tools text made of "[", `tools_json` and "]" (empty when `n_tools` is 0), and the widgets text made the same way from `widgets_json` and `n_widgets`. Finally it calls `end_turn()`.

`undo_changes()` does nothing when `session` is empty or `diff_count()` is 0. Otherwise it calls `web::js_call` on `web::WINDOW` with the method "confirm", the arguments text made of "[" and the JSON string "Undo all changes from this session?" and "]", and the tag `T_CONFIRM_UNDO`.

`undo_go()` calls `post_json` with the url "/api/undo", the tag `T_UNDO` and the body from `session_body`.

`on_undo()` handles the result of the undo request. It takes the response bytes with `web::fetch_take` on `web::ev_id()` into a buffer and frees them with `web::fetch_free`. When `web::ev_status()` is 0, or the bytes do not parse into `ed` with json::parse, it calls `add_msg` with the role "system" and the content "Undo failed". Otherwise, when the member "reverted" of the root is an array with at least one element, it calls `add_msg` with the role "system" and the content "Reverted ", the number of elements and " file(s)", then `diff_clear()` and `render_diff()`; when it is not, `add_msg` with the role "system" and the content "No changes to undo". The tools and widgets passed to `add_msg` are empty.

`accept_changes()` does nothing when `session` is empty or `diff_count()` is 0. Otherwise it calls `post_json` with the url "/api/accept", the tag `T_ACCEPT` and the body from `session_body`.

`on_accept()` calls `web::fetch_free` on `web::ev_id()`. When `web::ev_status()` is not 0, it calls `add_msg` with the role "system", the content "Changes accepted" and empty tools and widgets, then `diff_clear()` and `render_diff()`.

`interrupt_gen()` does nothing when `session` is empty. Otherwise it calls `post_json` with the url "/api/interrupt", the tag `T_IGNORE` and the body from `session_body`.

`steer_gen()` takes the text of the property "value" of the element "input", trimmed, and does nothing when it is empty or `session` is empty. Otherwise it sets that property to an empty text, calls `auto_resize()`, calls `post_json` with the url "/api/steer", the tag `T_STEERED` and a body that is a JSON object with the members "session_id" (the `session` text) and "message" (the text), and remembers the returned request id: it pushes the id on `steer_ids`, appends the text to `steer_texts` and pushes the new length of `steer_texts` on `steer_ends`.

`on_steer()` handles the result of a steer request. It takes the response bytes with `web::fetch_take` on `web::ev_id()` into a buffer, frees them with `web::fetch_free`, and finds the position of the request id `web::ev_id()` in `steer_ids`; when it is not there it stops. The remembered text is the part of `steer_texts` that ends at `steer_ends` at that position and starts at the end of the previous position, or at 0 for the first; it is copied into a local buffer. The id at that position becomes 0, and when every id is 0 all three lists are cleared. When `web::ev_status()` is not 0, the bytes parse into `ed` with json::parse and the "status" string of the root is "steered", it calls `add_msg` with the role "user", the content "[STEER: ", the text and "]" and empty tools and widgets; otherwise `add_msg` with the role "system", the content "Steer failed" and empty tools and widgets.

`copy_code(btn)` takes the handle `btn` of a copy button. It finds its closest ancestor with class "cb", and inside it the first "pre" element, and reads the property "innerText" of that "pre" (empty when there is none). It calls `web::clipboard` with that text, sets the text of `btn` to "copied", pushes `btn` on `copy_q` and starts a `web::timer` of 1200 milliseconds with the tag `T_COPY_RESET`.

`toggle_detail(btn)` takes the handle of a tool toggle button. It finds the first descendant with class "tool-detail" of the parent of `btn`, and stops when there is none. When the attribute "style" of that element contains the text "none" it sets its style "display" to "block", otherwise to "none".

`messages_click()` handles a click inside the element "messages". It takes the event target with `web::ev_target`. When its closest ancestor, itself included, matching ".cb-copy" exists, it calls `copy_code` with it and releases that handle after; else when its closest ancestor matching ".tool-toggle" exists, it calls `toggle_detail` with it and releases that handle after. It releases the target handle too, unless the target is the element "messages" itself (`web::same`).

`on_copy_reset()` takes the oldest handle of `copy_q`, advancing `copy_head`; when `copy_head` reaches the length of `copy_q` both are reset (the list cleared and the head 0). It sets the text of that handle to "copy".

`init()` registers these listeners with `web::listen` and no flags: on the element "input" the event "input" with `T_INPUT` and the event "keydown" with `T_KEYDOWN`; the event "click" on "send" with `T_SEND`, on "interrupt" with `T_INTERRUPT`, on "steer" with `T_STEER`, on "new-chat" with `T_NEW`, on "history-btn" with `T_HISTORY`, on "diff-toggle" with `T_DIFF` and on "messages" with `T_MSGS`. It also starts the date formatter: `web::js_call` on `web::js_global("Reflect")` with the method "construct", the arguments text `[{"$h":H},[[],{"year":"numeric","month":"numeric","day":"numeric","hour":"numeric","minute":"numeric","second":"numeric"}]]` where H is the handle `web::js_global("Intl.DateTimeFormat")` as a whole number, and the tag `T_FMT`.

`event(tag, kind)` handles the events of this module. `T_INPUT` calls `auto_resize()`. `T_KEYDOWN`: when `web::ev_key` is "Enter" and the `web::SHIFT` bit is not set in `web::ev_mods()`, it calls `web::ev_prevent()` and `send()`. `T_SEND` calls `send()`, `T_INTERRUPT` calls `interrupt_gen()`, `T_STEER` calls `steer_gen()`, `T_NEW` calls `new_chat()`, `T_HISTORY` calls `show_history()`, `T_DIFF` calls `toggle_diff()` and `T_MSGS` calls `messages_click()`. `T_STREAM` calls `on_chunk()` when `kind` is `web::EV_CHUNK` and `on_stream_end()` when it is `web::EV_FETCH`, and ignores other kinds. `T_UNDO` calls `on_undo()`, `T_ACCEPT` calls `on_accept()` and `T_STEERED` calls `on_steer()`, each only when `kind` is `web::EV_FETCH`. `T_IGNORE` calls `web::fetch_free` on `web::ev_id()` when `kind` is `web::EV_FETCH`, and does nothing for other kinds. `T_CONFIRM_UNDO` calls `undo_go()` when `confirmed()`, and `T_CONFIRM_CLEAR` calls `clear_history_go()` when `confirmed()`. `T_COPY_RESET` calls `on_copy_reset()`. `T_FMT` sets `date_fmt` to `web::js_handle(web::ev_id())` and calls `fill_times()`. `T_TIME` calls `on_time()`.

`act(verb, arg)` handles the queued actions of this module by the verb. "fill" sets the property "value" of the element "input" to `arg` and calls `focus_input()`. "undo" calls `undo_changes()`, "accept" calls `accept_changes()`, "diff" calls `toggle_diff()`, "clear_history" calls `clear_history()`, "new" calls `new_chat()`, "history" calls `show_history()` and "focus" calls `focus_input()`. "load" and "delete" read `arg` as a whole number with parse_int and call `load_chat` and `delete_chat` with it. "system" calls `add_msg` with the role "system", `arg` as the content and empty tools and widgets. "parse_diff" parses `arg` into `pd` with json::parse and, when that works, calls `diff_from_tool` with `pd` and its root.
